---
import FoundationsLayout from "../../components/FoundationsLayout.astro";
import SnippetBlock from "../../components/SnippetBlock.astro";
import { sourceSnippet } from "../../lib/snippet.ts";

const descriptorType = sourceSnippet("foundations-components-descriptor-type");
const notesDefinition = sourceSnippet("foundations-components-notes-definition");
const catalogType = sourceSnippet("foundations-components-catalog-type");
const catalogExample = sourceSnippet("foundations-components-catalog-example");
const registerFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-components-register");
const findFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-components-find");
const validateFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-components-validate-config");
const startFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-components-start");
const executableFn = sourceSnippet("foundations-components-executable-descriptor");
---

<FoundationsLayout
  slug="components"
  description="A component is one self-contained part of an application. Watershed describes reusable component kinds with versioned descriptors that hide config types behind decoders and start closures, while one catalog gives the host a common context, running value, port list, and precise lookup errors."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Why a components layer exists</h2>
    <p>
      Picture a board that two people build together while they watch it:
      one of them drops a notes panel on the left, puts a vote counter beside
      it, and connects the counter to the panel. Nobody wrote that
      arrangement in code. The people using the application made it, and it
      must look the same to everyone who opens the board tomorrow.
    </p>
    <p>
      A component is one part of a board like that: a notes panel, a counter,
      a task list. It keeps its own state in its own channel of the shared
      document, draws its own view, and knows nothing about what else is on
      screen. The same kind can appear many times with different settings —
      one counter that steps by 5, another that steps by 1 — and every copy
      of the board converges the same way the rest of watershed does.
    </p>
    <p>
      A part like that cannot be a function the author calls. The arrangement
      is data the users edit, and it outlives the session that made it. So
      the application must build each part from stored JSON, months later,
      from code that has changed since.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>descriptor</strong> is that recipe: everything needed to turn
      stored JSON into one running part, and back. Package a component once,
      and the rest of watershed can:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Rebuild it from a saved board, with the settings the user chose.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check a stored board before it runs anything, and name the exact part
        that is wrong.
      </li>
      <li>
        Run last year's version beside this year's, because each descriptor
        states its own format version.
      </li>
      <li>
        Let a user connect two parts that were written apart, because their{" "}
        <a href="/component-model/ports">ports</a> are listed data.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      One word runs through this sheet and the two after it: the{" "}
      <em>host</em>. The host is the program that embeds watershed and puts
      the board on screen, meaning your application shell, not a server. It
      owns the set of component kinds it can build, decides which versions it
      accepts, mounts the views, and supplies whatever context every component
      needs to start. Watershed defines the contract. The host is the program
      around it.
    </p>
    <p>
      That makes two jobs, and this sheet does them in that order. First you
      <em>use</em> components: the host holds a catalog of kinds and builds
      instances from stored config. Then you <em>write</em> one: a single
      component packaged so a host it never meets can build it. Stop after
      part one if you are assembling a board out of components somebody else
      wrote.
    </p>
    <p>
      This board carries through three sheets. Here it gets its parts:{" "}
      <a href="/component-model/ports">the next sheet</a> adds a task list and an
      activity log and wires them together, and{" "}
      <a href="/component-model/workspaces">the one after</a> saves the finished
      board so tomorrow's tab opens it.
    </p>
    <p>
      If your application ships one screen that you lay out yourself, you do
      not need any of this: declare your channels in{" "}
      <a href="/foundations/schema">the schema</a> and stop there. All three
      sheets are for the case where the user, not the author, decides what is
      on the page.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="g-block">
    <p class="annot">Part one · using components</p>
    <h2>One catalog, exact versions</h2>
    <p>
      A host holds one <strong>descriptor</strong> for each kind it can
      build. Treat a descriptor as opaque for now: it is one component kind
      packaged so the host can build it without knowing what it is. Part two
      writes one.
    </p>
    <p>
      A catalog keys each descriptor by its stable kind and integer format
      version. Several versions of one kind can coexist. Registering the same
      pair twice returns <code>DuplicateRegistration</code>.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={catalogType} />
    <p>
      Registration is where the notes panel and the counter land in the same
      structure for the first time.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={registerFn} />
    <SnippetBlock snippet={catalogExample}>
      <figcaption slot="caption" class="annot">
        notes and counter configs differ; the host contract matches
      </figcaption>
    </SnippetBlock>
    <p>
      Lookup requires the requested version, and that exactness buys two
      useful failure states. <code>NotRegistered(kind)</code> means the host
      knows no version of the kind at all.{" "}
      <code>UnsupportedVersion(kind, requested, available)</code> means it can
      name the versions it does know. A host can report the missing kind or
      the unsupported version without guessing which one went wrong.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={findFn} />
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>The catalog requires an exact version.</strong> Register an
        older descriptor when a host still supports it. The host owns
        migration and any fallback or code-loading policy.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Validation and startup stay separate</h2>
    <p>
      Checking that stored config is readable and actually running a component
      are different jobs. A manifest reader wants the first without the
      second.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>validate_config</code> runs the descriptor's decoder and stops
      there. A builder or manifest reader can check stored JSON without
      opening subscriptions or creating a running component.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={validateFn} />
    <p>
      <code>start</code> decodes the same JSON, then calls the typed start
      closure. Readiness waits for that closure's callback, so channel
      bootstrap and subscriptions can finish first.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={startFn} />
    <SnippetBlock snippet={executableFn} />
    <p>
      Because the two stay separate, the error tells you which side failed:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong><code>InvalidConfig</code></strong> reports the kind, version,
        and JSON decode error. The stored config is wrong.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>StartFailed</code></strong> reports the same identity
        plus the reason component code returned. The config was fine and the
        component could not start.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Input decoding, input handling, output validation, and cleanup keep
      their own errors too. The runtime never has to flatten a lifecycle
      failure into “component failed.”
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="g-block">
    <p class="annot">Part two · writing a component</p>
    <h2>A descriptor is the catalog boundary</h2>
    <p>
      From here on you are writing a component, not placing one. Everything
      above treated the descriptor as opaque. This is what is inside it, and
      why it has to hide what it hides.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two kinds run through this sheet: a notes panel and a counter. A host
      that holds both descriptors can build either one from stored JSON,
      without knowing what either one is.
    </p>
    <p>
      Their configuration is where the uniformity breaks. Notes needs a
      placeholder string. The counter needs an integer step. Gleam requires
      one type for all values in a catalog, and a broad config union would
      expose each component's private choices to the host.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>Descriptor(context, running)</code> keeps the config type out of
      its public type. The descriptor holds the decoder, callback-completed
      start closure, typed input handlers, and cleanup closure that know that
      type. The catalog sees only the two types every component in one host
      must share: what the host passes in, and what it gets back. It also
      carries erased port metadata, which{" "}
      <a href="/component-model/ports">the next sheet</a> picks up.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={descriptorType} />
  </div>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Each component keeps its config type</h2>
    <p>
      You still write ordinary typed Gleam inside a component. The notes
      definition decodes JSON into <code>NotesConfig</code>, then starts with
      that exact type. <code>component.descriptor</code> closes over both
      functions and removes the config type from the host-facing API.
    </p>
    <SnippetBlock snippet={notesDefinition} />
    <p>
      The two <code>String</code> parameters in the return type are the host
      contract in this test. A string context goes in and a string running
      value comes back. The counter descriptor hides a different config type
      behind the same two parameters.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h3>The workspace carries the definitions forward</h3>
    <p>
      Use this module to define descriptors, build catalogs, validate config,
      look up versions, start and stop instances, and execute typed handlers.{" "}
      <a href="/component-model/workspaces">The workspace layer</a> persists
      instances and assigns their child maps. The JavaScript component runtime
      reconciles those definitions, owns their lifecycle, and executes port
      deliveries. The host still mounts views and owns migration policy.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">The contract</span>
    <p>
      A descriptor hides one component's config type behind its decoder and
      start and cleanup closures. A catalog keeps those unlike definitions
      together under one host contract, looks them up by exact kind and
      version, and exposes their erased port metadata and typed handlers.
      Next: <a href="/component-model/ports">how those ports become a checked graph
      and an origin-aware dispatch trace</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</FoundationsLayout>
